  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 AM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nA closed mid-level low is advancing east across Colorado early this \nmorning as another potent wave dives southeast across southern \nSaskatchewan/Manitoba into the Northern Plains. Energy lifting \nnortheast ahead of the aforementioned low has generated another \nswath of rain and drizzle across the area. Limited instability \nhas kept rainfall rates low, generally 0.25\" per hour or less, \nand expect this to remain the case. Slightly higher rainfall \nrates and intermittent lightning have been observed across \nsoutheast Kansas. This will lift towards Anderson County over \nthe next hour or two. Will hold onto the Flood Watch for now, \nbut the overall potential for flooding is low and anticipate \nbeing able to cancel the Flood Watch early.\n\nContinued lift from the approaching mid-level low will lead to \nperiodic rain and/or drizzle for much of the day today. The northern \nstream wave absorbs the closed low and pushes a cold front through \nthe area by this evening. Gusty northwest winds behind the front \nusher in cooler and drier air for Friday and Saturday. CAA from \nbreezy north winds and cloud cover will keep highs in the 50s on \nFriday! Model soundings show steep low-level lapse rates leading to \nweak instability; REFS and HREF mean for MUCAPE is ~25 J/kg. As a \nresult, there is the potential for a couple of pop-up showers Friday \nafternoon and evening, which could mix down gustier winds aloft. The \nmost likely area for this looks to be east into Missouri, so have \nkept a dry forecast for now. A strong Canadian high pressure slides \noverhead Friday night into Saturday morning. Light winds and \nclearing skies set the stage for the first frost/freeze of the \nseason with lows forecast to be in the low to mid 30s. Areas north \nof Interstate 70 continue to have the highest likelihood of falling \nto or below freezing; NBM probabilities of reaching freezing are 40-\n65% in this area. Will likely need headlines frost/freeze headlines, \nbut uncertainty remains in exact areas of freeze vs frost.\n\nThe surface high moves east through the day Saturday, allowing \nsoutherly low-level flow to return and a warming trend to ensue \nearly next week. Highs will be near-normal by Sunday and back into \nthe 70s Monday through the middle of next week. Guidance is \nconsistent in a shortwave ejecting across the Plains on Monday, but \ndry low-levels favor this wave passing through dry. Better \nprecipitation chances come Wednesday-Thursday of next week as flow \nbecomes southwesterly ahead of a western US trough, although \nvariability among guidance is high.",
